// Places an opaque type cannot go, because nothing knows how large it is.
//
// struct-opaque is the other half: what an opaque type may do, which is be
// pointed at. This file is what it may not, and until now nothing asserted it --
// the type existed in the corpus only in the positions where it works.
//
// Every diagnostic here is the same code, because they are the same fact: the
// size is not known to this program, so no storage for one can be laid out. The
// message names the cause and the remedy, which is what tells them apart from
// the other four reasons a type has no size.

struct @opaque Handle {
}

// A field holds its type by value
struct Holder {
  h Handle   //~ ErrorNoSize:3 "is declared @opaque"
}

// So does a variable
fn inVariable() {
  mut h Handle   //~ ErrorNoSize:7 "is declared @opaque"
  return
}

// An array of them is worse than a field of one: the array itself is then a type
// with no size, so it is refused where it is written rather than where it is
// held. That is why the reference below is refused too -- a reference to an
// opaque type is the whole point of the type, but a reference to an *array* of
// them still needs the array to be a type, and it is not.
struct Slots {
  many [4; Handle]   //~ ErrorNoSize:8 "is declared @opaque"
}

fn behindRef(p &[4; Handle]) i32 {   //~ ErrorNoSize:17 "is declared @opaque"
  0
}
